---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro'
import Nav from '../../components/sections/Nav.astro'
import Footer from '../../components/sections/Footer.astro'
import SeriesNav from '../../components/blog/SeriesNav.astro'
import BallotHero from '../../components/blog/BallotHero.astro'
import Byline from '../../components/blog/Byline.astro'
import { postBySlug, formatPostDate, postSocialImage } from '../../data/blog'

const post = postBySlug('atoms-for-the-ballot')!
---

<Base
  title={`${post.title} — xNet`}
  description={post.description}
  image={postSocialImage(post).src}
  imageAlt={postSocialImage(post).alt}
>
  <Nav />

  <main>
    <BallotHero
      title={post.title}
      deck={post.description}
      date={formatPostDate(post.pubDate)}
      readingMinutes={post.readingMinutes}
      tags={post.tags}
    />

    <article
      class="prose prose-lg mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-16 dark:prose-invert prose-headings:tracking-tight prose-a:text-indigo-600 dark:prose-a:text-indigo-400"
    >
      <Byline post={post} />

      <p>
        In the autumn of 2010, Washington DC was about to let overseas voters
        cast real ballots through a website. Before it did, the city tried
        something brave. It put the system online for a mock election and
        invited anyone who liked to break in.
      </p>
      <p>
        A professor and three colleagues at the University of Michigan took
        up the offer. Within forty-eight hours they had near-complete control
        of the election server. They changed every vote and could read
        almost every secret ballot. They even found the building's security
        cameras on the same network and watched the server room from Ann
        Arbor. Then they left a calling card: after you voted, your browser
        played "The Victors", the Michigan fight song.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nobody running the election noticed for about a day and a half. What
        gave it away was a message on a mailing list, asking if anyone knew
        what tune the site played for successful voters. The city shelved the
        plan.
      </p>
      <p>
        That was sixteen years ago, and a great deal of clever cryptography
        has been written since. Yet in January this year, twenty-one computer
        scientists signed a letter saying what the field has said all along:
        internet voting is insecure, and nothing we know how to build can
        make it secure. The names on it include Ron Rivest, who is the R in
        RSA, and Bruce Schneier. These aren't people who are afraid of
        computers. So I went looking for why they're so sure, and came back
        with an awkward question about the software we make here.
      </p>

      <h2 id="five-things">Five things at once</h2>

      <p>
        The clearest account I found is a paper from MIT by Sunoo Park,
        Michael Specter, Neha Narula and Rivest. They list five things an
        election needs, and the list is worth walking slowly, because the
        whole problem is in how they rub against each other.
      </p>
      <p>
        First, the ballot is secret. That isn't a courtesy. If nobody can
        find out how you voted, nobody can pay you to vote their way, or
        threaten you if you don't. Before the secret ballot, buying votes was
        the main way elections were stolen.
      </p>
      <p>
        Second is a property with an ugly name, software independence. It
        means a hidden bug or a hidden change in the software must not be
        able to cause a hidden change in the result. You're allowed to use
        computers. You just can't be forced to take their word.
      </p>
      <p>
        Third, you can see that your ballot says what you meant before you
        cast it. Fourth, if someone finds an error, they can prove it to
        everyone else. And fifth, somebody actually checks. The authors have
        a nice line for a system that could be audited and never is: it's
        like keeping every receipt and never looking at the bill.
      </p>
      <p>
        Any one of these is easy. Now try them together, from a phone. To
        check that your vote counted, you need some kind of receipt. But a
        receipt that proves how you voted is a receipt you can sell, or be
        made to show to your boss. Cryptographers have clever ways round
        this, and they work on a whiteboard. The January letter's complaint
        is that in a real voter's hands they are confusing, and few voters
        bother.
      </p>
      <p>
        Suppose you do check, and your checker says your vote was changed.
        Now what? You can't show anyone without giving up your secret. The
        officials can't cancel an election because some people say their
        phones told them something. A check that can't end in proof, the
        letter says, adds no useful security at all.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then there's the phone itself. At a polling place the booth belongs
        to the election. At home the booth is your own device, which nobody
        has inspected, running whatever it picked up last month. Malware can
        show you one choice and send another. It can lie to the checking app
        as well.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two more things make it worse. An election happens once, so catching
        a fraud doesn't undo it; all you can do is run a different election
        later. And the attacker isn't a thief. It may be a state, and a state
        doesn't even need to change the result, only to leave enough doubt
        that the losing side won't accept it. What an election really
        makes isn't a winner. It's a loser who goes home.
      </p>

      <h2 id="the-bank">But I bank on my phone</h2>

      <p>
        Everyone says this, and it deserves a straight answer. Online banking
        isn't safe either. It's insured.
      </p>
      <p>
        Your bank knows exactly who you are, and every payment has your name
        on it. When a payment turns out to be fraud, the bank reverses it.
        When it can't, the bank or the shop or an insurer eats the loss, and
        the cost comes back to all of us as slightly higher prices. Fraud is
        a line in the budget. You can also audit the bank yourself, simply by
        reading your statement.
      </p>
      <p>
        A ballot allows none of that. Your name can't be on it. It can't be
        reversed. Nobody can make a country whole after a stolen election.
        And you can't read your own line in the count without breaking the
        secrecy that protects you.
      </p>
      <p>
        Hold on to this, because it matters later. Most of what we do with
        computers is shaped like the bank: named, fixable, and checkable by
        the person it belongs to. Voting is the strange one.
      </p>

      <h2 id="atoms">What atoms do for free</h2>

      <p>
        So why paper? Not because it's old, and not because election
        officials are sentimental. Look at what a marked paper ballot
        actually gives you.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can read it with your eyes. That sounds trivial until you notice
        that every electronic record needs a program to show it to you, and
        the program is the very thing in doubt. Paper is the only data format
        whose reader has no moving parts.
      </p>
      <p>
        You also can't edit paper from another continent. To change a paper
        ballot you have to be in the room with it. To change a million, you
        need a million acts, in thousands of rooms, in front of people who
        are there to watch. That takes a conspiracy, and big conspiracies
        leak. The MIT paper puts the other side plainly: one remote
        programmer, changing one line, could alter millions of electronic
        ballots in milliseconds. On paper, cheating costs something for every
        vote. In software the millionth vote is as cheap as the first.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is the sense in which electrons are less robust than atoms.
        Electrons copy for nothing, change without a trace, and can be
        reached from anywhere. Those are the same features that make them
        wonderful. The slowness that makes paper annoying is exactly what
        makes it hard to cheat with.
      </p>
      <p>
        None of this means counting by hand. Scanners count the paper, fast.
        Afterwards, officials pull a random sample of ballots and compare
        them with what the machines said. If the margin is healthy, a few
        hundred ballots can confirm a contest of tens of millions, with a
        known, small chance of being wrong. If the sample disagrees, you pull
        more, all the way to a full hand count if you must. It's called a
        risk-limiting audit, and America's National Academies asked for it
        in every state back in 2018, alongside paper for everyone and no
        marked ballots sent home over the internet. The machines give you
        speed, the paper keeps the machines honest, and a bit of statistics
        makes the honesty cheap.
      </p>
      <p>
        I owe Estonia a fair paragraph. It has voted online since 2005. In
        2023, for the first time, more than half its votes came in that way.
        Every citizen carries an identity card with a cryptographic key
        inside, and you can vote again to overwrite a vote someone pressured
        you into. Nobody has shown a stolen Estonian election. But when a
        team that included Alex Halderman, the professor from the DC story,
        studied the system in 2014, their finding wasn't that it had been
        hacked. It was that the design leans on trusting the voters'
        computers, the servers and the staff, and couldn't prove it
        <em>hadn't</em> been hacked. A small country with high trust has
        looked at that and decided to live with it. That's a real choice. It
        isn't a proof.
      </p>

      <h2 id="the-mirror">Now do us</h2>

      <p>
        Here's the awkward question. We build xNet, which hopes to move a lot
        of everyday software out of a few companies' data centres and into
        something closer to a commons: your data on your own machines,
        synced through servers that anyone can run. If the world's best
        security people can't make one vote safe online, what business do we
        have?
      </p>
      <p>
        There are three sharp versions of the doubt, and I've heard all of
        them. Isn't some software safer because attackers can't read it? Ours
        is open source; nobody even needs a decompiler. Isn't small safer?
        Ours isn't small. And if good people can read the code to find the
        holes, can't bad people read it too?
      </p>
      <p>
        Take secrecy first. Voatz was the phone voting app West Virginia used
        for real in 2018, and its code was closed. Three researchers at MIT
        pulled the app apart anyway, rebuilt enough of the server to study
        it, and found that an attacker could change, block or expose a
        person's vote. Hiding the code had delayed the bad news. It hadn't
        prevented it. Swiss Post went the other way in 2019 and published the
        code for its new voting system. Three outsiders found a flaw that
        allowed votes to be changed without detection, in the very part that
        was meant to prove nothing had been changed. They found it before an
        election rather than after, because they could read it.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the old rule holds. A Dutch linguist named Kerckhoffs wrote it
        down in 1883: a system should stay safe even when everything about it
        is public except the key. Keep your keys secret, and your passwords,
        and the details of a bug until it's fixed. Don't rest anything on the
        hope that nobody reads how the lock works. That hope is shrinking
        anyway. Software that reads software has got very good, very quickly,
        and it reads a compiled program nearly as happily as the source.
      </p>
      <p>
        But I won't pretend open code checks itself. Heartbleed sat in
        OpenSSL, the library guarding much of the web, for more than two
        years, while two full-time people looked after half a million lines
        on about two thousand dollars a year in donations. In 2024 somebody
        spent over two years befriending the one tired volunteer who
        maintained a small compression tool called xz, then slipped a back
        door into it. The piece that armed it never appeared in the public
        code at all, only in the packaged releases. One engineer caught it,
        because his logins were using more processor than they should.
        Openness made that catch possible. Luck made it happen. As for who
        wins the race between good readers and bad ones, the studies I could
        find say open and closed software come out about even on how bad
        their holes are. What differs is whether you're allowed to look.
      </p>
      <p>
        Now size. I counted. xNet is about seven hundred thousand lines of
        TypeScript across fifty-two packages, resting on nearly two hundred
        libraries we didn't write. When I ran a security audit on those
        libraries for this essay, it found eighty-five known serious
        problems, and until this month nothing in our pipeline was looking.
        Most will be unreachable from our code. I don't yet know which, and
        that's the point.
      </p>
      <p>
        The honest defence isn't that we're small. It's that the part which
        must be right is small. Every change you make in xNet is signed with
        your key and chained to the change before it. The server checks that
        signature on the way in, and every other device checks it again when
        the change arrives. So a server can lose your data, or refuse it, but under
        our standard settings it can't forge it. That's software independence
        in the MIT paper's sense, and it's why running your own server, or
        trusting a stranger's, isn't reckless. It also turns one big target
        into many small ones. Break into a company that holds everyone's
        data and you have everyone's. Break into one xNet server and you have
        one community's files, and still no way to forge a word in anyone's
        name.
        That's the difference between the line of code and the thousand
        rooms.
      </p>
      <p>
        And we're shaped like the bank, not the ballot. Your changes carry
        your name and can be undone. We never have to keep a record both
        secret and provable, so the impossible knot never gets tied.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here is where it stops being comfortable. All of that protects you
        from our servers. None of it protects you from our app. The app holds
        your key. If someone poisoned an update, the app could sign anything
        in your name, every check on every other device would pass, and
        nothing could tell the difference afterwards. That's the voter's
        phone again, and we haven't solved it any more than the election
        people have.
      </p>
      <p>
        We have four doors like that: the packages we publish, the desktop
        app's updates, the plugin marketplace, and the web app. The web app
        is the widest, because a website can hand out new code every time
        you load the page. If what you keep in xNet matters to you, use the
        desktop app, which asks before it updates. What we do have is a
        public, signed record of where each published package was built, and
        a second copy of the core rules, written in another language, that
        must agree with the first. What we don't have yet is a way for you to
        prove that the app you downloaded was built from the code you can
        read. Until this month we didn't even have a page telling researchers
        where to send a bug. We do now, along with a robot that proposes
        dependency updates and a check that fails when a new serious hole
        appears. The longer list of what's still open is in the sources
        below.
      </p>

      <h2 id="fast-where-its-safe">Fast where it's safe</h2>

      <p>
        So how does a democracy move forward, when everything else moves at
        the speed of light? I think the impatience is fair and the target is
        wrong.
      </p>
      <p>
        Almost everything around a vote is bank-shaped. Registering. Finding
        your polling place. Arguing about the question in public: cities from
        Barcelona outward run budgets and proposals on Decidim, which is open
        source. Tracking your postal ballot like a parcel. Publishing the
        results as signed data anyone can add up. Releasing the audit's
        evidence so a teenager with a laptop can redo the sums. All of that
        carries names, can be corrected, and gets better with more eyes. Send
        all of it down the fibre.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then keep atoms for the single step that has to be both secret and
        final. It's one moment, a few times a decade. It can afford a pencil.
      </p>
      <p>
        Most of the deciding people do together isn't even that hard. A
        housing co-op, a union branch, a software project, the members of one
        xNet server: they rarely need a secret ballot, and they're not facing
        a foreign spy agency. An open show of hands, signed so nobody can
        fake a hand, does the job, and that part is a solved problem. We've
        written the other half into our governance: xNet won't host a binding
        secret ballot. It isn't a feature we're behind on. It's a problem
        nobody has solved, and I'd rather say so than sell it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Light is for everything you can take back. For the one thing you
        can't, a piece of paper, a pencil, and a room with other people in
        it.
      </p>

      <hr />

      <h3 id="sources">Sources</h3>
      <ul class="text-base">
        <li>
          The Washington DC break-in is told by the people who did it:
          Wolchok, Wustrow, Isabel and Halderman,
          <a href="https://jhalderm.com/pub/papers/dcvoting-fc12.pdf" rel="noopener noreferrer"
            ><em>Attacking the Washington, D.C. Internet Voting System</em></a
          >
          (Financial Cryptography, 2012), read in full for this essay. The
          forty-eight hours, the thirty-six-hour delay, the cameras and the
          mailing-list message are all theirs. They also saw password-guessing
          from Iran, India, China and New Jersey in the logs, and say plainly
          that it was the internet's ordinary background noise, not an attack
          on the election, so I have left it out of the story.
        </li>
        <li>
          The five requirements, the line about one programmer and millions
          of ballots, and the banking comparison come from Park, Specter,
          Narula and Rivest,
          <a href="https://people.csail.mit.edu/rivest/pubs/PSNR20.pdf" rel="noopener noreferrer"
            ><em>Going from Bad to Worse: From Internet Voting to Blockchain Voting</em></a
          >
          (2020 draft, later in the <em>Journal of Cybersecurity</em>). The
          January 2026 letter is
          <a
            href="https://blog.citp.princeton.edu/2026/01/16/internet-voting-is-insecure-and-should-not-be-used-in-public-elections/"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            ><em>Internet voting is insecure and should not be used in public elections</em></a
          >, on Princeton's CITP blog, with all twenty-one names. The banking
          argument is made at more length by David Jefferson for
          <a
            href="https://verifiedvoting.org/publication/if-i-can-shop-and-bank-online-why-cant-i-vote-online/"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">Verified Voting</a
          >. "Software independence" is Rivest and Wack's term, from
          <a
            href="https://royalsocietypublishing.org/doi/abs/10.1098/rsta.2008.0149"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">a 2008 Royal Society paper</a
          >.
        </li>
        <li>
          Paper and risk-limiting audits are the recommendation of the US
          National Academies'
          <a href="https://www.nationalacademies.org/read/25120/chapter/7" rel="noopener noreferrer"
            ><em>Securing the Vote</em></a
          >
          (2018). "A few hundred ballots" is their own example and depends on
          a comfortable margin; close contests need far more.
        </li>
        <li>
          Estonia's system was examined by Springall and colleagues in
          <a href="https://jhalderm.com/pub/papers/ivoting-ccs14.pdf" rel="noopener noreferrer"
            ><em>Security Analysis of the Estonian Internet Voting System</em></a
          >
          (2014). The online majority in 2023 is reported by
          <a
            href="https://investinestonia.com/estonia-makes-history-with-i-voting-majority-and-record-female-presence-in-new-parliament/"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">Invest in Estonia</a
          >, a government promotion site, so treat the framing as friendly and
          the number as sound.
        </li>
        <li>
          Voatz: Specter, Koppel and Weitzner,
          <a
            href="https://www.usenix.org/conference/usenixsecurity20/presentation/specter"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"><em>The Ballot is Busted Before the Blockchain</em></a
          >
          (USENIX Security, 2020). The Swiss Post flaw was found by Sarah
          Jamie Lewis, Olivier Pereira and Vanessa Teague; the disclosure is
          at
          <a href="https://openprivacy.ca/work/swisspost-scytl-evoting/" rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >Open Privacy</a
          >. Swiss Post has since relaunched a rebuilt system for limited
          trials, with public code and a bug bounty, under the
          <a
            href="https://www.bk.admin.ch/bk/en/home/politische-rechte/e-voting.html"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">Federal Chancellery</a
          >.
        </li>
        <li>
          Kerckhoffs's rule and Shannon's shorter version, "the enemy knows
          the system", are summarised on
          <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kerckhoffs%27s_principle" rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >Wikipedia</a
          >. Heartbleed's dates and OpenSSL's staffing are from
          <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heartbleed" rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >its Wikipedia entry</a
          >, which cites the <em>New York Times</em>. The xz story is from the
          <a
            href="https://openssf.org/blog/2024/03/30/xz-backdoor-cve-2024-3094/"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">Open Source Security Foundation</a
          >
          and
          <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/XZ_Utils_backdoor" rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >Wikipedia</a
          >. "About even" is my reading of Guido Schryen's
          <a
            href="https://www.researchgate.net/publication/220891308_Security_of_Open_Source_and_Closed_Source_Software_An_Empirical_Comparison_of_Published_Vulnerabilities"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">comparison of published vulnerabilities</a
          >, which is careful and more than a decade old. Machines that find
          bugs: Google's
          <a
            href="https://projectzero.google/2024/10/from-naptime-to-big-sleep.html"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">Big Sleep</a
          >
          is the example I trust; I have left vendors' headline numbers out.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Decidim" rel="noopener noreferrer">Decidim</a>
          began in Barcelona in 2016 and is free software.
        </li>
        <li>
          Our side is in the open. Signing and checking live in
          <code>packages/sync/src/change.ts</code>, the server's check in
          <code>packages/hub/src/services/node-relay.ts</code>, and each
          device's own check in <code>packages/data/src/store/store.ts</code>.
          "Standard settings" matters: there is a compatibility switch,
          <code>allowUnsignedReplication</code>, that turns the guarantee off,
          and it is off by default. The line counts are from
          <code>git ls-files</code> on the day of writing. The eighty-five
          advisories are high or critical findings from
          <code>pnpm audit --prod</code>, now held by
          <code>scripts/check-dependency-audit.mjs</code> against a committed
          baseline so the number can only fall. The new
          <a href="https://github.com/crs48/xNet/blob/main/SECURITY.md" rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >security policy</a
          >
          says where to send a bug. The secret-ballot rule is in
          <a href="https://github.com/crs48/xNet/blob/main/GOVERNANCE.md" rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >GOVERNANCE.md</a
          >.
        </li>
        <li>
          Still open on the day this was published, and findable in
          <a
            href="https://github.com/crs48/xNet/tree/main/docs/explorations"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">our explorations</a
          >: no reproducible builds, so nobody outside can yet confirm that a
          release matches the source; no answer for the web app beyond "prefer
          the desktop"; a threat model begun in
          <a href="https://github.com/crs48/xNet/blob/main/docs/explorations/0134_%5B_%5D_BAD_ACTOR_ATTACKS_AND_SECURITY_THREAT_MODEL.md" rel="noopener noreferrer">0134</a>
          and never finished; the end-to-end review of how a change travels,
          <a href="https://github.com/crs48/xNet/blob/main/docs/explorations/0307_%5B_%5D_SECURITY_OF_NODE_AND_CHANGE_FLOW.md" rel="noopener noreferrer">0307</a>, likewise; desktop
          releases that fall back to an unsigned-by-Apple build when our
          signing secrets are absent; and a newer, quantum-resistant
          signature scheme that is written but not yet wired into the change
          log. The plan for closing them is exploration 0463.
        </li>
      </ul>

